Here’s how you can clearly explain the difference between WCF Relay and Hybrid Connections when your Cloud team asks these questions during a Service Bus request:


---

✅ 1. WCF Relay – What It Is

🔸 Purpose:

Designed to expose on-premises WCF services to the outside world securely via Azure Relay.

The full WCF stack (contracts, bindings, behaviors) is supported.


🔸 Use Case:

If your application uses WCF and needs to be accessed over the internet without exposing a public IP or opening inbound firewall ports.


🔸 Key Features:

Protocol: WCF (NetTcpRelayBinding, WS2007RelayBinding)

Communication: Real-time, direct, bi-directional

Binding required in app: Yes

Requires full WCF integration



---

✅ 2. Hybrid Connections – What It Is

🔸 Purpose:

A lighter, simpler option to connect cloud apps to TCP endpoints (e.g., HTTP/HTTPS) running on-premises.

Part of Azure App Service but powered by Azure Relay under the hood.


🔸 Use Case:

If your cloud app (like a Web App or Function App) needs to access an on-premise HTTP endpoint (like a REST API), without needing WCF.


🔸 Key Features:

Protocol: TCP-based, usually HTTP/HTTPS

Simpler setup (no full WCF support needed)

No WCF bindings or contracts

Used mostly with App Services, Logic Apps, Azure Functions

Not bi-directional (client → server only)



---

🆚 WCF Relay vs Hybrid Connections – Summary Table

Feature	WCF Relay	Hybrid Connections

Protocol	WCF (NetTcpRelayBinding, etc.)	TCP (mostly HTTP/HTTPS)
Target	On-premises WCF services	On-premises HTTP APIs/Apps
Communication	Full-duplex (bi-directional)	Unidirectional (client to server)
Setup Complexity	High (requires WCF config)	Low (simple endpoint and listener)
Use with App Services	❌ No	✅ Yes
Use for legacy WCF apps	✅ Best suited	❌ Not supported
Network requirements	Only outbound port 443 needed	Only outbound port 443 needed



---

📝 How to Respond to the Cloud Team:

> "We need a Service Bus Topic/Queue and not WCF Relay or Hybrid Connections. Those are different Azure Relay features.

WCF Relay is only needed if we want to expose an on-prem WCF service directly to the cloud.

Hybrid Connections is used mainly for cloud apps to connect to on-prem services (usually REST APIs) without WCF.


Our request is specifically for Service Bus (Messaging) to implement pub/sub or message queuing, which is a different service from Relay."




